# --- W2-TAG words ---
# M-TAG — shape polymorphism.  A GOLF value is a LIST iff it is an address inside
# the heap; everything else is an INT, including the -1/0 flags \eq/\rlt produce
# (0xFFFF… is far above the heap) and every small literal (far below it).  The
# two bounds live in runtime cells — heap base 0x4F0034, span 0x4F0038 — so W6
# can move or grow the heap by rewriting two stores and nothing else.
# Top-level init: the prelude is prepended source, so plain tokens run at program
# start, before any user code.  W6/M-MEM moved that init to the "W6 allocator"
# block below, where it asks the kernel for the bounds instead of naming them —
# both cells are still written before anything can allocate or call T, because
# every allocation happens in a word body and user code runs last.
# T  v -> 0 (list) / 1 (int).  Zero-is-true, so `T[…]` runs the LIST path.
# (v-base) <u span is -1 exactly for a heap address and 0 otherwise; 1\radd maps
# that to 0/1.  A leaf: no scratch, hence no spill frame, hence callable anywhere
# — including from inside D's own dispatch, where scratch is live.
:T5177396@\rsub 5177400@\rlt 1\radd;
# K  list s fn -> list   new[i] = fn(list[i], s)      broadcast list ⊙ scalar
# G  s list fn -> list   new[i] = fn(s, list[i])      broadcast scalar ⊙ list
# Two words rather than one plus an argument swap, because fn need not commute —
# ∸ and its order are exactly the point.  Structure is M's: X/Y frame, s0 list,
# s1 index, s3 fn, s4 result, s7 the parked scalar; the length header is written
# outside the `0\eq[…]` guard so the empty list yields an empty list; every
# pointer/index/flag step is a raw op.  fn sees user data and so stays \exec'd.
# W4A: s7 is a 64-bit cell now, so the parked scalar keeps its full width — the
# old wart where `-3 ⍳4 +` broadcast 4294967293 (a 32-bit round trip through
# scratch) is GONE, and a negative or >= 2^32 broadcast scalar survives intact.
:K X5177464\store5177496\store5177440\store5177440\fetch\fetch"1\radd A"5177472\store\store0 5177448\store5177440\fetch\fetch0\eq[{5177440\fetch5177448\fetch I5177496\fetch5177464\fetch\exec5177472\fetch5177448\fetch8\rmul\radd8\radd\store5177448\fetch1\radd5177448\store5177448\fetch5177440\fetch\fetch\rlt1\radd}]5177472\fetch Y;
:G X5177464\store5177440\store5177496\store5177440\fetch\fetch"1\radd A"5177472\store\store0 5177448\store5177440\fetch\fetch0\eq[{5177496\fetch5177440\fetch5177448\fetch I5177464\fetch\exec5177472\fetch5177448\fetch8\rmul\radd8\radd\store5177448\fetch1\radd5177448\store5177448\fetch5177440\fetch\fetch\rlt1\radd}]5177472\fetch Y;
# D  a b fn -> c   the binary shape dispatcher, and the calling convention M-VEC
# will route the bare + - * ⌈ ⌊ templates through: fn is a quotation address.
# a and b are NEVER parked in scratch — they stay on the data stack, so the
# int,int path hands fn its operands at full 64-bit width and the list paths hand
# Z/K/G the very words they want.  `&` reaches under the flag being built, so the
# only state is s3 (fn) and s2 (the shape code 2*T(a)+T(b): 0 ll · 1 li · 2 il ·
# 3 ii), both covered by X/Y.  Guards are `code k\rsub[…]` — zero, so true, only
# for its own k — and exactly one fires; Z/K/G restore s2 as they leave, so the
# guards after the one that ran still read the code and not someone's garbage.
:D X5177464\store&T2\rmul&T\radd5177456\store5177456\fetch[5177464\fetch Z]5177456\fetch1\rsub[5177464\fetch K]5177456\fetch2\rsub[5177464\fetch G]5177456\fetch3\rsub[5177464\fetch\exec]Y;
# The user-facing polymorphic operators, bytes 0xC0..0xC4 (REGISTRY.md §1.2).
# Three tokens each: ′atom auto-wraps the raw scalar op in a thunk and D picks
# the shape.  The definition name is the high byte itself — `:\vadd` is `: 0xC0`.
:\vadd \ref\radd D;
:\vsub \ref\rsub D;
:\vmul \ref\rmul D;
:\vmin \ref\rmin D;
:\vmax \ref\rmax D;
# --- W2-CHAIN words ---
# Tacit combinators (M-CHAIN).  ∘ compose · ⇉ pipeline · ⑂ fork.  A quotation is
# just a code address (what ′ pushes), and the whole load segment is RWX, so ∘
# BUILDS ONE AT RUNTIME with the byte-store op `,`: it assembles a 39-byte thunk
#   [PROLOGUE][48 B8 f:u64][FF D0][48 B8 g:u64][FF D0][EPILOGUE]
#     sub rbp,8; pop [rbp]  |  mov rax,f; call rax  |  mov rax,g; call rax
#     |  push [rbp]; add rbp,8; ret
# — the exact prologue/epilogue a compiled word gets, so ⍎ calls it like any
# other word: the PROLOGUE parks ⍎'s return address (`call` pushes it on the
# DATA stack) on the return stack at rbp, f and g then run as plain words over
# the data stack, and the EPILOGUE hands the address back and rets.  The two
# imm64 operands are written with `!` (4 bytes: the address, all < 2^31) then a
# second `!` for the four zero high bytes; every opcode byte goes out with `,`.
# Those are MACHINE-CODE writes at their own natural widths and have nothing to
# do with list cells, so W4A left every `,` and `!` in the body alone — only ∘'s
# three scratch slots moved to the new bank.
#
# Thunks come from a dedicated CODE ARENA at 0x4D0000 (bump pointer 0x4F003C),
# never from the list heap: an address inside the heap bounds would be
# indistinguishable from a list to the shape test, whereas 0x4D0000 sits below
# the variable bank and above the compiler's ceiling — and, since W6/M-MEM,
# below the program break, i.e. below the heap base — so a composed quotation
# classifies as an int like every other ′ address.  ∘ is X/Y-wrapped (it parks f,
# g and the thunk's start address in s1/s2/s3) so it may be called from inside a
# map or a fold; each call bumps the arena by 39 bytes.
#
# The arena is BOUNDED and the bound is enforced here.  0x4D0000..0x4DFFFF is
# 65536 bytes = 1680 thunks at 39 bytes each; the 1681st would start at
# 0x4E0017, which is inside the USER VARIABLE BANK at 0x4E0000 (../REGISTRY.md
# §3).  Without this check a program that composes in a loop silently overwrites
# its own →x/←x cells with machine code and keeps running — the thunks stay
# callable and the exit status stays 0, so the corruption is invisible.  Nothing
# can be freed (a thunk's address may have escaped anywhere), so the only honest
# answer is to stop: 65536-39 = 65497 is the last legal offset, so an offset of
# 65498 or more dies here rather than wrapping into user data.
# The message goes to fd 2 via ⎈ and the status is 1 — a compiled program writes
# its real output to fd 1, so a diagnostic must never land there.
:∘5177404@65498\rlt[
 “GOLF: compose arena exhausted“5177440\store
 2 5177440\fetch4\radd 5177440\fetch@ 1\sys_
 10 5177448\store 2 5177448 1 1\sys_
 1.]
